For PostgreSQL troubleshooting, sar is one of the most valuable Linux tools because it answers:

Is the problem CPU, memory, disk, network, swapping, paging, or load?

Most DBAs only use top, but sar provides historical and subsystem-specific metrics.

1. CPU Analysis
Overall CPU
sar -u 1 10


Example:

%user    %system   %iowait   %idle
33.42    30.91      0.01     35.66


Meaning:

Field	Meaning%user	User-space workload (Postgres queries)
%system	Kernel activity
%iowait	Waiting for disk
%idle	Unused CPU
Interpretation

Good query workload:

user=80%
system=10%


Storage bottleneck:

user=10%
system=5%
iowait=70%


Kernel-heavy workload:

user=30%
system=30%


(your spike looked like this)

Per CPU
sar -P ALL 1 5


Find:

one overloaded core
uneven CPU distribution

Example:

CPU0 95%
CPU1 2%


Often indicates single-thread bottleneck.

2. Memory Analysis
Memory usage
sar -r 1 10


Example:

kbmemfree
kbavail
%memused


Useful to verify:

actual available memory
Linux cache effects
Page Faults
sar -B 1 10


You already ran this.

Example:

fault/s
majflt/s

Important

Minor faults:

fault/s


Usually harmless.

Major faults:

majflt/s


Dangerous.

Your system:

fault/s ≈ 5000
majflt/s = 0


Healthy.

Swapping
sar -W 1 10


Example:

pswpin/s
pswpout/s


Healthy:

0
0


